  "jd_text": "Manager of Applied AI Architecture, Partnerships\nSan Francisco, CA | New York City, NY | Seattle, WA\n\nAbout Anthropic\n\nAnthropic's mission is to create reliable, interpretable, and steerable AI systems. We want AI to be safe and beneficial for our users and for society as a whole. Our team is a quickly growing group of committed researchers, engineers, policy experts, and business leaders working together to build beneficial AI systems.\n\nAbout the role\n\nAs the Manager of the Partnerships Applied AI Solutions Architect team, you will drive adoption of frontier AI by enabling deployment of Anthropic's products (Claude for Enterprise, Claude Code, API) through our Global and Regional System Integrators, cloud partners (AWS, GCP, Azure), and strategic technology partners.\n\nYou will build and lead a team of Partner Solutions Architects, establish processes and best practices for partner-led pre-sales engagements, and represent Anthropic as the technical lead on its most important partnerships. In collaboration with Sales, Partnerships, Product, and Engineering, you will help partners incorporate leading-edge AI into their practices, accelerate indirect revenue, and execute long-term GTM strategy while maintaining our best-in-class safety standards.\n\nResponsibilities\n\n    Team Leadership & Development: Hire, manage, and mentor a team of Partner Solutions Architects. Set goals, run reviews, and coach each team member toward high productivity and career growth.\n    Strategic Technical Partnership: Act as the senior technical thought partner to Anthropic's GTM partnerships team. Co-build partner strategy with aligned GTM leadership, drive key programs, and align cross-functional stakeholders (Sales, Product, Engineering) behind partner outcomes.\n    Partner Enablement & Ecosystem: Embed your team with GSI and cloud partner technical teams to enable their AI practices, troubleshoot, and evangelize Anthropic in their developer communities. Represent Anthropic at partner events (GSI workshops, AWS/GCP summits, hackathons) and contribute technical content and thought leadership.\n    Joint Solution Development: Lead partners in identifying high-value, industry-specific GenAI applications. Develop joint solutions and codify reference architectures and best practices to accelerate time to deployment.\n    Customer Deal Support: Own the technical portion of partner-led pre-sales engagements. Intervene directly on strategic deals where partners are the primary delivery vehicle, providing deep solution architecture guidance.\n    Product Feedback: Gather and validate feedback on Anthropic's products from partner deployments and deliver it to Product and Engineering to inform roadmap and partner strategy.\n\nYou may be a good fit if you have\n\n    7+ years in technical customer-facing or partner-facing roles (Solutions Architect, Sales Engineer, Partner SE, TAM).\n    3+ years managing pre-sales or partner-facing technical teams; comfortable building foundational teams in ambiguous, fast-moving environments.\n    Track record building and scaling partnerships with GSIs (e.g., Accenture, Deloitte, TCS, Infosys) and/or cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure).\n    Deep understanding of partner-led selling and delivery: indirect revenue models, enablement at scale, and joint GTM motions.\n    Technical depth in enterprise AI deployments: LLM architecture, prompt engineering, evaluation, API integrations, and production use cases.\n    Exceptional communication and executive presence; able to build trusted relationships with C-suite, partner leadership, and engineering teams alike.\n    A love of teaching and mentoring, and a passion for advancing safe, beneficial AI.\n\nStrong candidates may also have\n\n    5+ years leading partner-facing SA teams through hypergrowth, including developing both senior and junior talent.\n    Direct experience helping GSIs or consultancies build their AI/ML practice - enablement programs, certification paths, joint solution development.\n\nCompensation\n\nThe annual compensation range for this role is listed below.\n\nFor sales roles, the range provided is the role's On Target Earnings (\"OTE\") range, meaning that the range includes both the sales commissions/sales bonuses target and annual base salary for the role.\n\nAnnual Salary: $315,000 - $380,000 USD\n[# APPS_RG_EXEC_SUMMARY_TARGETING_CAP targeting-only; not proof]\n",
  "briefing": "# Anthropic - Manager of Applied AI Architecture, Partnerships briefing packet\n| Manager of Applied AI Architecture, Partnerships | $315,000\u2013$380,000 USD | Reports to Partnerships (partner-GTM context) |\n\n## JD Complement\nAnthropic\u2019s public posture implies this is not simply a field-architecture leadership need. It is an operating-scale need: converting a safety-centered frontier-model product into repeatable, partner-delivered enterprise outcomes.\n\nThe central tension is deployment consistency versus ecosystem reach. Anthropic needs partners to extend technical coverage, industry context, and change-management capacity without allowing solution quality, security practices, or\nresponsible-use standards to fragment by partner.\n\n## Company DNA & Operating Model\nAnthropic is a San Francisco-based public benefit corporation founded in 2021. Its identity is explicitly safety-and-research led, with Claude as its flagship product family.\n\nIts archetype is product-led enterprise AI with a platform-mediated, ecosystem-assisted delivery layer. It wins through the perceived quality, reliability, and trustworthiness of Claude, while partners help translate a general-purpose model\nplatform into enterprise workflows and industry deployments.\n\nThe operating challenge is to preserve a consistent Anthropic implementation standard while external services, cloud, and technology partners increasingly influence how customers evaluate, deploy, and adopt Claude.\n\n## Company Strategy & Operating Pressure\nAnthropic\u2019s public-benefit structure is material commercial context. The company is not positioned solely around model capability; it makes reliability, interpretability, steerability, and beneficial deployment part of its institutional\nidentity.\n\nClaude is presented both as an AI assistant for knowledge work and as an enterprise-grade capability. That creates pressure to make adoption measurable beyond initial access: technical configuration, organizational rollout, enablement, and\nscaling all matter to commercial durability.\n\nThe decision-rights shift is from one-off technical partnership support toward reusable deployment patterns. Partner motions need enough central governance to protect product feedback loops and implementation quality, while remaining\nflexible enough for industry-specific delivery.\n\n## Leadership & Stakeholder Map\nDario Amodei and Daniela Amodei are publicly identified as Anthropic founders and former OpenAI members. Their founding context reinforces the company\u2019s safety-centered identity.\n\nThe most consequential internal stakeholder set is likely to include:\n- Partnerships and commercial leadership, which need partner capacity and repeatable joint motions.\n- Product and engineering teams, which need high-signal deployment feedback rather than isolated feature requests.\n- Enterprise adoption and customer-facing teams, which need clear boundaries between product capabilities, implementation guidance, and partner-delivered services.\n- Safety, policy, and governance functions, whose standards shape what \u201cgood\u201d enterprise deployment looks like.\n\n## AI, Data, Platform, Architecture Signals\nAnthropic describes its work as building reliable, interpretable, and steerable AI systems. For enterprise architecture, that means technical positioning must connect capability claims to controls, operational confidence, and responsible\nuse.\n\nAnthropic\u2019s Claude Enterprise administrator guidance defines deployment as four phases: technical setup; change management and launch; enablement and training; and scaling adoption. This is a strong signal that implementation is treated as\nan adoption lifecycle, not a single integration event.\n\nThe same enterprise guidance includes Claude Code seat configuration. This suggests that enterprise rollout increasingly crosses business-user and developer-user populations, raising the value of shared administration, enablement, and\ngovernance patterns.\n\n## Partnership / Ecosystem Motion\nAnthropic publicly positions its partner ecosystem across cloud, technology, and services partners. The motion is therefore broader than referral relationships: partners can influence infrastructure choice, solution design, implementation,\nand adoption.\n\nAccenture publicly presents its Anthropic relationship around enterprise AI deployment, pairing Anthropic\u2019s models with Accenture\u2019s industry and technology expertise. This is a concrete indicator that joint value propositions will need both\ntechnical credibility and vertical relevance.\n\nThe key ecosystem operating question is how to turn joint solution work into reusable assets: reference architectures, enablement materials, implementation guardrails, and feedback channels that improve future partner deployments.\n\nA March 2026 report described a $100 million Claude Partner Network commitment, including partner-portal, training, and sales-playbook infrastructure. If sustained, that increases the need for disciplined technical enablement and common\ndelivery standards across the network.\n\n## Recent Events & Urgency\nEnterprise adoption materials now explicitly emphasize change management, training, and scaled usage alongside technical setup. That raises the bar from successful demos to sustained organizational deployment.\n\nThe reported Claude Partner Network investment creates urgency around partner readiness. As ecosystem capacity expands, inconsistent technical qualification, uneven solution quality, or unstructured product feedback become more expensive\noperating problems.\n\nCompetitive pressure in enterprise AI is increasingly about who can operationalize models through trusted cloud, technology, and services channels\u2014not solely who can demonstrate model performance.\n\n## apps_rg Positioning Themes (targeting only, not proof)\n- Building repeatable technical plays for partner-led enterprise deployments.\n- Translating AI platform capability into governed production architectures.\n- Creating partner enablement that spans technical setup, adoption, and scale.\n- Balancing solution velocity with security, responsible-use, and quality standards.\n- Converting field and partner signals into structured product-roadmap feedback.\n- Developing industry-specific solution patterns without creating bespoke-services sprawl.\n- Leading technical teams through ambiguous ecosystem and commercialization changes.\n\n## apps_lic Outreach Angles (targeting only, not proof)\n- Anthropic\u2019s deployment model appears to treat adoption as a full lifecycle, not an implementation handoff.\n- The cloud, technology, and services partner mix makes technical consistency a strategic issue, not merely an enablement task.\n- The Accenture relationship is a useful example of the need to combine model capability with industry delivery expertise.\n- Claude Enterprise and Claude Code create a shared enterprise-administration and adoption conversation across business and developer users.\n- The public-benefit and safety orientation makes scalable implementation governance especially relevant.\n\n## Do Not Use As Proof\nThe JD and this briefing are targeting inputs only. Candidate claims must come from the governed resume and proof graph.\n[# APPS_RG_EXEC_SUMMARY_TARGETING_CAP targeting-only; not proof]\n",
